Strawberry Video Player
=======================

Plays the videos in ~/Videos (alphabetical, non-recursive).

Output
  HDMI connected at start -> HDMI, sound over HDMI
  otherwise               -> built-in LCD, sound over the onboard speaker
  If HDMI is unplugged during playback the video moves to the LCD at the
  same position.

Keys (Cardputer keyboard, or any attached USB keyboard)
  Tab              switch LCD <-> HDMI
  q  Space  Return play / pause
  z  x  Del        previous video (wraps)
  c  f             next video (wraps)
  w                back 30 seconds
  e                forward 30 seconds
  s  d             volume down / up
  a                mute
  l                toggle loop one video / loop all videos (default: loop one)
  h                help (on the LCD while HDMI is playing)
  Esc              quit

Usage
  strawberry-video-player              play ~/Videos
  strawberry-video-player DIR          play DIR
  strawberry-video-player FILE         play FILE's folder, starting at FILE

How HDMI works
  Preferred: VLC with Raspberry Pi's DRM video output. For files the Pi's hardware
  decoder supports, it feeds the display directly (zero-copy), which is what makes
  1080p smooth on this hardware. VLC needs the display to itself, so the desktop
  (lightdm) is stopped while it plays and started again afterwards (about 10
  seconds to come back). A small watcher restarts the desktop even if the player
  is killed. The LCD shows an info page (title, EDID name, progress, volume) and
  is kept awake while the player runs. There is no on-screen text on HDMI itself.

  This needs permission to stop and start lightdm.service. The package does NOT
  grant that by itself. To allow it, install a polkit rule such as:

    polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
        if (action.id == "org.freedesktop.systemd1.manage-units" &&
            action.lookup("unit") == "lightdm.service" &&
            (action.lookup("verb") == "stop" || action.lookup("verb") == "start") &&
            subject.isInGroup("video")) { return polkit.Result.YES; }
    });

  in /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/50-strawberry-video-player.rules (as root).
  Without permission the player plays HDMI in a window on the desktop (mpv,
  software decoding), which works but is less smooth at 1080p.

  Hardware or software decoding is chosen per file from its stream header
  (ffprobe). The Pi's hardware decoder handles 8-bit 4:2:0 H.264, profiles up to
  High, at level 4.2 or lower. Anything else (for example a level 5.1 encode with
  10 reference frames, which shows a black or gray picture in hardware) is played
  with the software decoder automatically: this works for 1080p24 at up to about
  20 Mb/s but uses about 250% of the four cores, and switching between a hardware
  file and a software file restarts VLC for a couple of seconds.

  Encoding for the Pi (smoothest, lowest CPU): H.264, High profile or lower, level
  4.1 or 4.2, 4 reference frames, 8-bit 4:2:0, 1080p24-30, up to ~20 Mb/s. With
  ffmpeg:  -c:v libx264 -profile:v high -level 4.1 -refs 4 -pix_fmt yuv420p

  LCD: mpv with software decoding and an early downscale to 320x170, with
  on-screen messages.

Environment variables
  STRAWBERRY_DEBUG=1           log to stderr (player logs in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR)
  STRAWBERRY_HDMI_BACKEND=     vlc or mpv (desktop window); default: vlc if allowed
  STRAWBERRY_HDMI_VO=          gpu|wlshm renderer for the mpv desktop-window mode
  STRAWBERRY_VLC_ARGS=         extra options appended to the VLC command line (testing)
  STRAWBERRY_HDMI_MODE=        HDMI mode WxH@Hz (default 1920x1080@30: the display's 1080p mode
                               with the nearest refresh rate; many displays offer only 50/60 Hz)

Configuration file (optional): ~/.config/strawberry-video-player.conf
  hdmi_mode = 1920x1080@30     # same as STRAWBERRY_HDMI_MODE
  display_aspect = 16:10       # same as STRAWBERRY_DISPLAY_ASPECT
  Some monitors have a 16:10 (or 4:3) panel and stretch the 1920x1080 signal to fill it, so
  everything looks too tall. Set display_aspect to the panel's shape: the player then
  letterboxes the video inside the signal and the monitor's stretch makes it look right.

Command line
  strawberry-video-player --start 11:40 FILE    start at a position (SECONDS or MM:SS)

Notes
  Videos must be readable by the user running the player.
  Memory: the display shares a CMA pool with the decoder. Raise it with
  dtoverlay=vc4-kms-v3d,cma-128 in /boot/firmware/config.txt (recommended).
